## Bug Identification

The bug is a **data race** on `c.count`. The `Increment()` method performs a non-atomic read-modify-write (`c.count++`), and `GetCount()` reads `c.count` without any synchronization. When 1000 goroutines call `Increment()` concurrently, multiple goroutines can read the same value, increment it, and write back the same result — causing lost updates. This is why the final count is sometimes lower than 1000.

## Why It Causes Wrong Totals

`c.count++` is not atomic in Go. It compiles to roughly:
1. Read `c.count`
2. Add 1
3. Write back to `c.count`

If two goroutines interleave at step 1 (both read the same value), they both write back the same incremented value, and one increment is lost. Under heavy load, this happens frequently.

## Fix

Use `sync/atomic` to make the increment and read atomic operations.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
    "sync/atomic"
)

type Counter struct {
    count int64
}

